# range

`range` is a special Go keyword for iterating over various data structures. It is much safer and more idiomatic than using manual indices.

## range over Slice

```go
nums := []int{10, 20, 30, 40, 50}

// index and value
for i, v := range nums {
    fmt.Printf("nums[%d] = %d\n", i, v)
}

// Index only
for i := range nums {
    fmt.Println("index:", i)
}

// Value only (ignore index)
for _, v := range nums {
    fmt.Println("value:", v)
}
```

## range over Map

```go
scores := map[string]int{
    "Alice": 95,
    "Bob":   82,
    "Carol": 88,
}

for key, value := range scores {
    fmt.Printf("%s: %d\n", key, value)
}
// Map key order is NOT guaranteed!

// Keys only
for name := range scores {
    fmt.Println(name)
}
```

## range over String

When iterating a string, range produces **runes** (not bytes), so it supports Unicode:

```go
for i, r := range "Héllo" {
    fmt.Printf("index %d: %c (U+%04X)\n", i, r, r)
}
// index 0: H (U+0048)
// index 1: é (U+00E9)  <- 2 bytes, index jumps to 3
// index 3: l (U+006C)
// index 4: l (U+006C)
// index 5: o (U+006F)
```

**Note:** The index is the byte position, not the character position!

## range over Array

```go
colors := [3]string{"red", "green", "blue"}

for i, color := range colors {
    fmt.Printf("%d: %s\n", i, color)
}
```

## range over Channel

```go
ch := make(chan int, 3)
ch <- 1
ch <- 2
ch <- 3
close(ch)

for v := range ch {
    fmt.Println(v)  // 1, 2, 3
}
// Loop stops automatically when channel is closed
```

## All range Forms

```go
// Slice: index and value
for i, v := range slice { }

// Slice: index only
for i := range slice { }

// Slice: value only
for _, v := range slice { }

// Map: key and value
for k, v := range m { }

// Map: key only
for k := range m { }

// String: byte index and rune
for i, r := range str { }

// Channel
for v := range ch { }
```

## Example: Character Frequency Counter

```go
func charFrequency(s string) map[rune]int {
    freq := map[rune]int{}
    for _, r := range s {
        freq[r]++
    }
    return freq
}

func main() {
    freq := charFrequency("hello")
    for r, count := range freq {
        fmt.Printf("%c: %d\n", r, count)
    }
    // h: 1, e: 1, l: 2, o: 1
}
```

